= Introduction

This report evaluates methods for detecting hardware faults in language model
inference hardware. Bit flips are injected into the weights of Qwen3-0.6B
(bfloat16), and the report compares how well random and optimized test
patterns detect them.

= Experiments

== Experimental setup

Permanent hardware faults are simulated by flipping bits in the stored model
weights, as a cosmic-ray strike or a defective memory cell would. Two fault
classes are distinguished: flips in the mantissa bits (small numerical
distortions) and flips in the exponent bits (large jumps in magnitude). Each
fault flips k bits.

A *test pattern* is an input prompt fed to the model; the model's generated
text is the test response. The 100 test patterns used here are drawn randomly
from the benchmarks under test and cover three tasks: 40 factual questions
(TriviaQA-style), 20 grade-school math word problems (GSM8K-style), and 40
text completions (WikiText-style).

A fault counts as detected if the faulty model's response differs from the
fault-free response on at least one test pattern;
responses are compared token by token. All decodes are greedy
and therefore deterministic, so identical inputs always yield identical
outputs.

== Degradation under faults

As a starting point, faults of increasing severity are injected and benchmark
accuracy is measured after each injection. Figure 1 shows accuracy on standard
benchmarks (HellaSwag, MMLU) and text quality (WikiText perplexity) as
a function of the expected number of flipped bits per fault, spanning five
orders of magnitude. The dashed line marks the fault-free level.

