== Random test patterns

Random test patterns are generated by randomly sampling prompts from a
predefined corpus of natural-language inputs (the 100 patterns of
Section 2.1). This keeps the patterns within the natural input space of the
model and avoids meaningless or nonsensical sequences that would result from
sampling individual tokens independently. The sampled prompts are then used
as test inputs to evaluate whether the faulty model produces outputs that
deviate from those of the fault-free model.

A randomly selected pattern detects a fault when the input happens to
activate fault-sensitive model computations, resulting in an observable
deviation in the model's output. The fraction of faults detected by at least
one test pattern defines the *coverage*; the number of patterns decoded per
fault is the *pattern budget* n.

== Gradient-guided pattern search (HotFlip)

Gradient-guided test patterns are generated by starting from n patterns
drawn from the same 100-pattern set and iteratively editing them with a
HotFlip-style greedy search (Ebrahimi et al. 2018), so the final pattern
set consists of n edited patterns;

The search signal is the model's first-token margin:
a pattern is a promising detector when the most probable first token
of the faulty model deviates from that of the fault-free model.
Each candidate edit replaces one token with another. Following HotFlip, the
effect of a substitution is estimated from a single backward pass instead of
decoding every candidate. A substitution that replaces token a with token b
at position j is represented by a vector v in the one-hot input space, and
the substitution with the largest first-order change of the margin loss J is
chosen:

$
  max_(i, j, b) ((partial J) / (partial x_(i j)^(b)) - (partial J) /
    partial x_(i j)^(a)) = max_(i, j, b) nabla_x J dot v_(i j)^(b),
$

where x is the one-hot input representation. The most promising substitution
is verified on the real decode and accepted if it improves the signal, and
this repeats until the search stops. The search stops as soon as one
pattern's margin signals the fault; the signal is then verified by fully
decoding that pattern and comparing the response with the reference token by
token, so the detection criterion is the same as in the random baseline.

== Naive optimized patterns

Naive optimized test patterns are generated by the same procedure as the
gradient-guided search, with the ranking step replaced by a forward-only
substitution search. Starting from the same n patterns (the recorded runs
use the same random start draws as the gradient-guided search), each
position of a pattern is exchanged for the top-k tokens of that position's
softmax distribution; every candidate is scored by the first-token margin
on the real bf16 decode, and the best-scoring candidate is accepted
greedily. No gradient information is used. As in the gradient-guided
search, the search stops at the first margin signal, and the refined
pattern is verified on a full 64-token decode. The generation budget is
matched to the gradient-guided search (13,000 row-equivalents per fault),
so the comparison isolates the effect of gradient ranking at equal
compute. Results are reported in Section 2.6.


== Results

The methods are compared at equal pattern budget n, the number of test
patterns a method may use. Each (method, budget) combination is a separate
experiment on 385 faults per class, a sample size that bounds the coverage
error at ±5 points with 95 percent confidence (Leveugle et al. 2009); every
fault is evaluated
R = 5 times with independently drawn pattern budgets (random n-subsets of
the 100 patterns for the random baseline, randomly drawn start patterns for
the search), so the per-fault detection rate measures the variance across
pattern draws. Test coverage is the pooled detection rate over both fault
classes and over the repeats, with 95 percent confidence intervals; the
per-fault detection rate is the fraction of faults detected in at least
one of the R repeats. The best method per budget is set in bold.

